   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the insurance law in relation to extending provisions of the property/casualty insurance availability act",
    "basePrintNo": "S5806",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5806-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§2342, 2305, 2344, 3425, 2328, 2329 & 5412, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5806\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the insurance law in relation to\nextending provisions of the property/casualty insurance availability\nact\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis bill would extend several provisions of the Insurance Law that\npromote the stability of the property/casualty insurance market.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Insurance L. § 2342 to extend the\nexpiration of Insurance L. §§ 2307(c), 2308, 2310(a), 2316, 2320,\n2323, 2326, 2335, and 2336(b) for three years to June 30, 2020. These\nprovisions relate to: (i) the ability of the Superintendent of\nFinancial Services to waive certain prior approval requirements\n(section 2307(c)); the imposition of prior approval (section 2308);\n(iii) certain filing requirements for insurers and rate service\norganizations where prior approval is not required (section 2310(a));\n(iv) the prohibition of anti-competitive behavior for insurers and\nrate service organizations (section 2316); (v) enforcement and\npenalties provisions where prior approval is not required (section\n2320); (A) policy profitability and rates of return where prior\napproval is not required (Section 2323); (vii) the evaluation of\ncompetitive rating provisions where prior approval is not required\n(Section 2326); (viii) the prohibition of surcharges for certain\naccidents and traffic infractions of emergency responders (section\n2335); (ix) and; (x) rate reductions for automobiles with passive\noccupant restraint systems (section 2336(b)).\n\nSection 2 would amend Insurance L. § 2305(f) to extend, through June\n30, 2020, the provisions in Insurance L. § 2305(a) relating to\nexceptions from prior approval for certain insurance rates.\n\nSection 3 would amend Insurance L. § 2344(h) to extend, through June\n30, 2020, the applicability of the flexible rate limitations in\nproblem markets for certain commercial property/casualty insurance\nrates.\n\nSection 4 would amend Insurance L. § 3425(m) to extend, through June\n30, 2020, certain provisions dealing with nonrenewal requirements for\nautomobile insurance.\n\nSection 5 would amend Insurance L. § 2328 to extend, through June 30,\n2020, certain provisions relating to motor vehicle insurance rates.\n\nSection 6 would amend Insurance L. § 2329 to extend, through June 30,\n2020, certain provision relating to motor vehicle insurance rates.\n\nSection 7 would amend Insurance L. § 5412(g) to extend, through June\n30, 2020, provisions allowing the New York Property Insurance\nUnderwriting Association (\"NYPIUA\") to write certain coverage upon a\ndetermination by the Superintendent that coverage is unavailable in a\nparticular market.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThe provisions in Sections 1 through 6 of the bill were similarly\nextended in 2003, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2011 and 2014 (L.2003, c. 85;\nL.2006, c. 115; L.2007, c. 268; L.2008, c. 136; L.2011, c. 102;\nL.2014, c. 78).\n\nThe provision in Section 7 was extended in 2003, 2004, 2005, 2006,\n2007, 2008, 2011 and 2014.\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe aforementioned Insurance Law provisions provide well-established\nconsumer protections regarding rate regulation for property/casualty\ninsurance, and also facilitate the ability of property/casualty\ninsurers to continue to make certain types of insurance readily\navailable to New Yorkers. Extending these provisions will allow this\nexisting framework to continue.\n\nThe bill also would extend the authority of the New York Property\nInsurance Underwriting Association (NYPIUA) for an additional three\nyears. This extension will promote the stability, availability and\naffordability of property/casualty insurance in the state by extending\nthe Superintendent's authority to authorize NYPIUA to write coverage\nfor an underserved market.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediate.\n",
